+# Moving the installed app to a new origin
+
+Old origin: `https://dailygrind.example.com`, new origin: `https://app.dailygrind.example.com`.
+They share the registrable domain `example.com`, which the browser requires for a migration.
+
+| File | Deploy to | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `manifest.webmanifest` (has `migrate_to`) | **old** origin | Lets installed copies find out about the move without visiting the new site first |
+| `.well-known/web-app-origin-association` | **old** origin | The old origin gives permission for `https://app.dailygrind.example.com/` to take over |
+| `new-origin/manifest.webmanifest` (has `migrate_from`) | **new** origin, served at `/manifest.webmanifest` | The new app names the old app as the one it replaces |
+| `index.html`, `icons/` | both | Same page on both origins; the script checks `location.origin` |
+
+The `.well-known` file must be served as JSON (`application/json`). Make sure the static host doesn't block dotfile paths.
+
+## What moves and what doesn't
+- **The browser moves it (Chrome/Edge 149+):** the installation itself, including OS shortcuts, taskbar/dock pins and home-screen icons.
+  `behavior: "suggest"` asks the user. Switch it to `"force"` to make it required: the user must update or remove the app.
+- **Not moved by the browser:** localStorage, IndexedDB, cookies and permissions.
+  User preferences (localStorage keys starting with `dg:`) go across in the URL fragment of the
+  "Open the new app" link on the old origin. The new origin imports them, never overwrites values it already has,
+  and removes the payload from the URL. Account data on the server stays with the account.
+- **Firefox/Safari:** the old origin shows a banner that links to the new origin, carrying the preferences.
